 been which has been  communicated  is very much another calgary's police  chief says the directive will be  enforced but not for two weeks adding  that the issue eliminates a growing  distrust between officers and members of  the police commission our members are  paying very close attention to the  social media accounts of  of individual counselors  and the quantity of anti-police  content that's being sort of posted  shared or liked is is really causing  concern to the members there's gonna  have to be uh serious discussion perhaps  the sheer number of officers who are  defying this it's going to be incredibly  challenging the calgary police  commission is standing by its directive  maintaining that sooner or later all  police service members will have to  comply heather eurix west global news  calgary  ahead on global national beyond the  headlines journalism with a very  personal connection  [Applause]  another first since the pandemic began  worshipers from around the world  traveling to jerusalem for palm sunday  parishioners lined the streets for a  procession before prayers echoed in the  church during mass palm sunday marks the  start of the holy week for christians  well the recent meetings in rome between  pope francis and indigenous delegations  from canada attracted dozens of  journalists to report on the historic  event but for one correspondent from the  aboriginal people's television network  covering the visit hit very close to  home nitu garcia reports  do you feel you were speaking on behalf  of the 250 little voices that can no  longer speak for themselves aptn  journalist tina house interviewing to  come loops to sweat mccook p roseanne  kasmir in rome this represents the  little moccasins here the 215  unmarked graves that were found in  kamloops she traveled with the  delegation to cover reconciliation  meetings with pope francis with her own  piercing perspective everyone here has  been affected by residential schools one  way or another including her tina's  grandmother was forced into a  residential school her mother went to a  boarding school in my career as a  journalist now i've literally probably  interviewed hundreds of survivors  and it has affected me in a way that i  literally feel like i'm paying a piece  of my soul every time i tell a story  tina's now 15 year career got underway  with a gruesome case she intimately told  the detailed story of her missing cousin  only revealing at the end it was her  relative in a half hour special on  missing and murdered indigenous women  and girls which won an amnesty  international human rights award in 2010  all that work culminating in a watershed  moment earlier this month this is the  very first time that our voices are  being heard the metis  we have been so disenfranchised our  whole lives because of our mixed race a  proud moment for her people more than  two decades after her late father hailed  a national metis hero won this  aboriginal achievement award in 2001  and tina adding to the house family  hardware not long after her return to  canada  [Applause]  [Music]  one day to rest and boom hit the ground  and now i win national reporter for the  canadian screen awards dad if you're  here now  thank you  is this you and i love you  as the first indigenous journalist to  win that award she's making history  while also covering it  nitu garcia global news  next reading the fine print the big  donation of hundreds of very small books  [Music]  well as the saying goes don't judge a  book by its cover even if it's a teeny  tiny cover a couple of rare book  collections are catching the eye of  officials at the jewish library in  montreal  but as phil carpenter explains in order  to truly appreciate these volumes you'll  need a magnifying glass  these are probably among the smallest  books that have ever existed  oh my gosh there's a million reasons why  they're so special this is just one in a  collection of miniature books that are  about to go on display at the jewish  public library in montreal the volumes  are donated by lily toth who arranged to  have them given to the library upon her  death  there are more than a thousand i was  blown away by them i'd never seen  anything i've seen miniature books like  one here one there but to see an entire  room toth a holocaust survivor passed  away a year ago at 96. she made her way  to montreal in 1956 after the hungarian  revolution a story on dr christian  howard notes that this collection is  remarkable there are bibles and books  with bible verses that are several  hundred years old there are a lot of  more recent books which  collectors call mass market minis there  are books of poetry fine literature  children's literature cookbooks  there's even an english yiddish  dictionary from around the early 1900s  you could  put in your pockets if you're walking  around and you can't remember the  english word you can't remember the  yiddish word there's also a collection  of works of shakespeare  perhaps the smallest this  ultra-micro-miniature book of the lord's  prayer written in seven languages  measuring less than five millimeters  howard says that nowadays people buy  miniature books for artistic reasons and  collectors are rare which is why they  were surprised to learn of another  collector lily's friend daisy gross also  a holocaust survivor i love books i read  a lot  and i enjoy reading  it takes me to different world each time  you read  she owns 1300 miniature books  she doesn't know what will happen to her  collection when she passes that's not my  problem  it's up to my children gross hasn't  ruled out donating them to the library  something officials there would no doubt  be grateful for  phil carpenter global news montreal  and that is global national for this  sunday night i'm sophie louie tonight  your canada is the aurora borealis  lighting up the sky over iqaluit nunavut  we would love to see your corner of the  country so please keep sending your